Subject: [PATCH 0012] PCG: one reduction per fixed-count DIC-CG iteration

Use preconditioned Chronopoulos/Gear CG only for DIC with
minIter > 0 && minIter >= maxIter. Keep the original loop for other controls
or preconditioners. Keep the initial guess, normalisation, initial residual,
min/max/checkConvergence predicate, singularity test, residual-field hooks,
setFinished and solverPerformance meanings. The caller GAMG starts the coarse
correction at zero and only prints the returned performance when requested;
no GAMG changes are needed. Exactly minIter steps are taken absent breakdown,
just as before (35 for the intended case).

Reference: Ghysels & Vanroose, Hiding global synchronization latency in the
preconditioned Conjugate Gradient algorithm, Parallel Computing 40 (2014),
224-238, doi:10.1016/j.parco.2013.06.001, Algorithm 2, lines 4-13 and Eq. (3)
(the preconditioned Chronopoulos/Gear form, not the deeper pipelined form).

Let u=M^-1*r, w=A*u, gamma=r.u, delta=w.u. Reduce (gamma,delta,L1(r))
together with FixedList<solveScalar,3> and sumOp<solveScalar> on the matrix
communicator, as in GAMGSolver::scale. Use p=u+beta*pOld and
s=w+beta*sOld=A*p. First eta=delta, then beta=gamma/gammaOld and
eta=delta-beta*gamma/alphaOld; alpha=gamma/eta. In exact arithmetic,
eta=p.A*p=delta-beta^2*etaOld, so this is the same DIC-CG iteration.
The x and r updates are x+=alpha*p and r-=alpha*s as before.

F3 rounding change (not bit-identical): the direct (A*p).p reduction is
replaced by (A*u).u minus beta*gamma/alphaOld, which can suffer cancellation;
s is formed by a vector recurrence instead of directly evaluating A*p. The
same p, x and r recurrences therefore receive different rounded operands.
Packing the reductions may also change MPI's summation tree. L1 is still a
sum of abs(r_i), never a squared norm or an inferred recurrence value.

One startup reduction prepares the first step. After each update, the next
u and w (if needed) are computed and the updated r's L1 norm is packed with
the next step's products. The check thus sees exactly the same quantity at
the same iteration as before, without a lag. The final reduction needs only
L1; the other two entries are zero and no unused preconditioner or Amul is
performed. A complete N-step solve has N+1 such reductions, versus 3N in the
old loop, plus unchanged setup reductions. For N=35: 36 versus 105. There
are still N preconditioner applications and N iteration matrix multiplies.
The reduction is blocking; no nonblocking overlap is claimed.

With contraction enabled, dot-product sums, s=w+beta*sOld and the unchanged
p/x/r multiply-add/subtract expressions may use FMA on a suitable target.
No flags are changed. GCC 13.3 defaults to -ffp-contract=fast with
-std=c++17; explicit -ffp-contract=off prevents contraction. F3 Cd and
breakdown/stability validation on the 512-rank case remain necessary.

